WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.362
Quantization lowers the numerical precision used for model weights and sometimes other data.

00:00:06.362 --> 00:00:14.919
It reduces memory and bandwidth pressure, making local inference practical on hardware that cannot support the original precision.

00:00:14.919 --> 00:00:27.424
The savings can improve model loading and allow more layers to remain on an accelerator, but scales, metadata, activations, runtime buffers, and context cache still consume memory.

00:00:27.424 --> 00:00:31.154
Precision labels describe tradeoffs, not guaranteed outcomes.

00:00:31.154 --> 00:00:36.639
FP16, BF16, INT8, and low-bit formats require compatible hardware and runtimes.

00:00:36.639 --> 00:00:44.318
GGUF and safetensors are containers used in different workflows; neither extension alone establishes quality or precision.

00:00:44.318 --> 00:00:49.364
Tokenizers, templates, architecture metadata, and model revisions must remain compatible.

00:00:49.364 --> 00:00:57.042
Q4 is often a useful baseline, while lower levels favor fit and higher levels favor fidelity.

00:00:57.042 --> 00:01:06.037
The right choice depends on model architecture, available VRAM or RAM, context, batch, offload, kernel support, and task sensitivity.

00:01:06.037 --> 00:01:14.155
A model that barely fits may run worse than a smaller or more compact option with headroom.

00:01:14.155 --> 00:01:16.130
Choose through testing.

00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:21.614
Compare several variants with the same runtime, prompts, settings, and hardware.

00:01:21.614 --> 00:01:28.854
Measure speed and memory, evaluate factual and task quality, repeat trials, and record the configuration.

00:01:28.854 --> 00:01:38.288
Quantization succeeds when it preserves the quality required by the workload while delivering stable, maintainable performance on the target system.
